8,"21) Other things remaining the same, marginal utility theory implies that a rise in the price of a good",A) increases the consumer surplus.,B) decreases the consumer surplus.,C) leaves consumer surplus unchanged.,D) has an effect on consumer surplus that cannot be determined.,E) decreases consumer surplus by the amount equal to the total utility of all goods consumed before the price rise but not after the price rise.,B,Predictions of Marginal Utility Theory,False
8,22) The paradox of value as it applies to diamonds and water can be explained by,A) distinguishing between utility and price.,B) water having a high level of marginal utility relative to diamonds.,C) water having a low price relative to diamonds.,D) the fact that utility can't be measured.,E) distinguishing between total utility and marginal utility.,E,Predictions of Marginal Utility Theory,False
8,"23) The high price of diamonds relative to the price of water reflects the fact that, at typical levels of consumption,",A) the total utility of water is relatively low.,B) the total utility of diamonds is relatively high.,C) the marginal utility of water is high.,D) the marginal utility of diamonds is relatively low.,E) None of the above is true.,E,Predictions of Marginal Utility Theory,False
8,24) Which one of the following statements is true?,"A) Since consumers of a good consume this good until the price equals the value of the last unit, they clearly get no benefit from consuming this good.",B) Consumers choosing between goods X and Y will set MUX = MUY.,C) The value of a good to a consumer is measured by the price.,D) Consumer surplus on a unit of a good will never be zero.,"E) If the marginal utility per dollar of good X is less than the marginal utility per dollar of good Y, then the consumer should shift some spending from X to Y.",E,Predictions of Marginal Utility Theory,False
8,25) The paradox of value,A) is that values are the same for different people.,"B) is that economists can calculate marginal utility, even though utility is unmeasurable.",C) is resolved by an appeal to the law of diminishing total utility.,D) remains unresolved by economists.,E) is resolved by an appeal to the difference between marginal utility and total utility.,E,Predictions of Marginal Utility Theory,False
8,"26) Childcare workers often get paid fairly low wages, yet it is said that they have ""the most important job in the world."" This paradox can be resolved by",A) distinguishing between price and value.,B) distinguishing between marginal utility and total utility.,C) noting that childcare yields no utility since it is a service.,D) noting that marginal utility does not diminish for childcare.,E) none of the above.,B,Predictions of Marginal Utility Theory,False
