18,"10) If the present value of $500 to be received in one year is $463, the interest rate is",A) 5 percent per year.,B) 8 percent per year.,C) 10 percent per year.,D) 20.8 percent per year.,E) 37 percent per year.,B,Mathematical Note: Present Value and Discounting,False
18,"11) If the rate of interest is 15 percent per year, the present value of the $200 to be received in two years is",A) $170.00.,B) $185.00.,C) $173.91.,D) $264.50.,E) $151.23.,E,Mathematical Note: Present Value and Discounting,False
18,"12) If the present value of the $1,000 to be received in one year is $800, the interest rate is",A) 25 percent per year.,B) 8 percent per year.,C) 10 percent per year.,D) 12 percent per year.,E) 125 percent per year.,A,Mathematical Note: Present Value and Discounting,False
18,"13) If the present value of $100 received one year from now is $80, what is the annual interest rate?",A) 8 percent,B) 10 percent,C) 20 percent,D) 25 percent,E) 50 percent,D,Mathematical Note: Present Value and Discounting,False
18,"14) The present value of a future sum of money is the amount that, if invested today, will grow","A) as large as that future sum, given the interest rate.",B) as large as that future sum.,"C) as large as that future sum, less taxes payable.","D) as large as that future sum, if the rate of interest is zero.",E) at a constant rate forever.,A,Mathematical Note: Present Value and Discounting,False
18,"15) Assuming r is the rate of interest, to compute the present value of a dollar to be received a year from today, you",A) multiply the dollar by r.,B) divide the dollar by (1 - r).,C) multiply the dollar by (1 + r).,D) divide the dollar by r.,E) divide the dollar by (1 + r).,E,Mathematical Note: Present Value and Discounting,False
