22,"4) According to the law of diminishing returns, along the aggregate production function an additional unit of",A) capital produces more output than an additional unit of labour.,B) labour decreases output.,C) labour produces more output than the previous unit.,D) labour produces less output than the previous unit.,E) labour increases the real wage rate.,D,How Potential GDP Grows,False
22,"5) Refer to Figure 22.3.1. The country of Kemper is on its aggregate production function at point W in the above figure. If the population increases with no change in capital or technology, the economy will",A) move to point such as Y.,B) remain at point W.,C) move to point such as X.,D) move to point such as Z.,E) either remain at point W or move to point X.,C,How Potential GDP Grows,False
22,"6) If the money wage rate is $15.00 an hour and the price level is 120, the real wage rate is",A) $8.50 an hour.,B) $10.75 an hour.,C) $12.50 an hour.,D) $15.00 an hour.,E) $18 an hour.,C,How Potential GDP Grows,False
22,"7) If the money wage rate is $10.00 an hour and the price level is 60, the real wage rate is",A) $16.67 an hour.,B) $18.75 an hour.,C) $10.00 an hour.,D) $12.50 an hour.,E) $6.00 an hour.,A,How Potential GDP Grows,False
22,"8) If the real wage rate is $10.00 an hour and the price level is 60, the money wage rate is",A) $16.75 an hour.,B) $18.50 an hour.,C) $10.00 an hour.,D) $6.00 an hour.,E) $16.67 an hour.,D,How Potential GDP Grows,False
22,"9) When the quantity of labour demanded exceeds the quantity of labour supplied, the real wage rate",A) rises to eliminate the labour market shortage.,B) falls to eliminate the labour market surplus.,C) rises to eliminate the labour market surplus.,D) falls to eliminate the labour market shortage.,E) does not change but the money wage rate rises to eliminate the labour market shortage.,A,How Potential GDP Grows,False
22,"10) In the labour market, an increase in labour productivity ________ the real wage rate and ________ the level of employment.",A) raises; increases,B) raises; decreases,C) lowers; increases,D) lowers; decreases,E) raises; does not change,A,How Potential GDP Grows,False
22,11) Refer to Figure 22.3.2. The equilibrium real wage rate is,A) $10 an hour.,B) $15 an hour.,C) $20 an hour.,D) any wage rate above $15 an hour.,E) any wage rate below $15 an hour.,B,How Potential GDP Grows,False
22,12) Refer to Figure 22.3.2. The equilibrium quantity of labour is,A) 100 billion hours.,B) 150 billion hours.,C) 200 billion hours.,D) 50 billion hours.,E) 250 billion hours.,B,How Potential GDP Grows,False
